  "body": "The entire source tree uses `constantly` in exactly one file, originally added in #6377.\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/blob/8a87b4435a736cd42454cad7e57b65ec911f01fa/synapse/storage/databases/main/events_worker.py#L33\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/blob/8a87b4435a736cd42454cad7e57b65ec911f01fa/synapse/storage/databases/main/events_worker.py#L153-L160\r\n\r\nIn other places, we use the stdlib [`enum`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/enum.html?highlight=enum#module-enum) module instead. For instance:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/blob/e24ff8ebe3d4119d377355402245947f7de61c00/synapse/handlers/state_deltas.py#L25-L28\r\n\r\nI would like us to use the enum approach and ditch constantly, for three reasons:\r\n1. consistency across the source tree.\r\n2. mypy knows what an enum is; it don't know what a NamedConstant is. E.g. `reveal_type(EventRedactBehaviour.AS_IS)` yields `synapse/handlers/message.py:1407: note: Revealed type is \"Any\"`.\r\n3. I prefer using a well-known tool from the stdlib rather than the lesser-known constantly.\r\n\r\nTask list:\r\n\r\n- [ ] replace import of `constantly` with equivalent imports from stdlib `enum`\r\n- [ ] redefine the `EventRedactBehaviour` type in terms of `enum`\r\n- [ ] remove [these lines](https://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/blob/8d156ec0ba17d848581f18aa40ebfd76dda763d4/mypy.ini#L250-L252) from mypy.ini.\r\n- [ ] run the linter script: in particular, run mypy. Fix any errors that show up.",
